    "Commitment/Consistency": {"Explanation": "The email does not explicitly reference prior behavior or choices to push further action, but it encourages the reader to take action based on the perceived value and urgency of the offer, which could be interpreted as a form of commitment/consistency."},
    "Liking": {"Explanation": "The email does not use familiarity, flattery, or personalization to build trust or rapport with the recipient, so this principle is not present."},
    "Social proof": {"Explanation": "The email includes testimonials and positive feedback from other participants, which serves as social proof to encourage the recipient to join the program and take advantage of the opportunity themselves. This is a clear example of using social proof to persuade the reader."}
}

